# Evaluation tooling and boundary

The current source provides `prepare_evaluation` and `score_predictions`. No evaluation has been executed. Human-adjudicated evaluation is not measured; comparative acceptance is incomplete.

`claim-auditor eval --protocol LOCK.json --input <verified-snapshot-bundle> --out <new-directory>` prepares a source-only packet. It writes `protocol-copy.json`, `packet.json` and `PREPARED.json`; every arm remains `not_run`. It does not call a provider or create a fictitious inference result. The source-only snapshot is the operator-staged, independently controlled input; no labels, curator gold or adjudication rationale are opened by packet preparation.

`prepare_dev_packet(snapshot_root, out) -> EvaluationPacket` is the separate keyless DEV entrypoint. It uses the same `_build_packet` as admitted preparation and writes only `packet.json`/`DEV.json` in a fresh directory outside source. DEV metadata explicitly denies admission, heldout, human and comparative acceptance; no LOCK or rights/access/custody receipt is fabricated. The original `_check_lock` remains unchanged for real admitted evaluation. Locked preparation checks sealed source-control metadata before a loader reads source bytes.

The strict `EvaluationLock` in `contracts.py` requires admission attribution/time, source and gold seal digests, code digest, taxonomy/matcher/packet versions, explicit required arm/model names, rights/custody receipts and bounded token/time/money ceilings. The implementation checks code/source identity and version/cap consistency. Receipt strings and admission fields are operator attestations; actual human provenance and access isolation need independent verification. The code digest covers installed Python source, not a proven dependency lock. Dependency pinning and resolved provider model/context conformance remain activation obligations.

Required arm names are `product_full_index`, `packet_candidate`, `packet_grep`, `live_extraction`, and `live_single_pass`. Product full-index is a separate utility row. All four paired arms must use identical serialized packet bytes and digest; they may reopen only admitted spans for integrity. The packet builder uses raw README code-span anchors and an independent static text candidate index, never candidate extractor/model outputs or gold labels. Windows are deterministic, merged, at most 1 KiB each and 48 KiB total, with at most 8 KiB mandatory scope metadata. Omitted windows and incomplete scope remain explicit. The packet carries no absence certificate; absent text alone cannot support an accusation. Provider token limits still override byte limits before any future call.

`claim-auditor score --predictions <closed-run-or-predictions.jsonl> --gold <gold.jsonl> --out <new-directory>` writes descriptive `RESULTS.json`, `items.jsonl` and `results.md`. Closed runs are first reopened by the sole citation service. Their explicit accusation fields become prediction rows; only the scorer maps exact snapshot identity to curator family IDs, after inference closure. Raw JSONL inputs must use the closed `PredictionRow` and `GoldAtom` schemas, including explicit accused predicate IDs. Gold and labels never go back into inference.

The matcher is `raw-span-predicate/1`: exact snapshot/file digest, raw half-open span, quote digest, kind and structured predicates. It permits only distribution-name normalization (`-`, `_`, `.` and case) for dependency subjects. It preserves conditions, negation, numbers and referents. Matching is one-to-one; ambiguous gold matches and duplicate/extra predictions cannot inflate recall. Every gold atom remains in the item table as a matched result, omission or explicit pipeline failure. Screened abstentions, invalid output, oversize input, citation failure and execution failure stay visible.

Metrics include extraction precision/recall, predicate-level accusation precision, atom-level defect recall, verified precision, both harmful error directions, partial correctness, unknown appropriateness/overuse, addressable share and answerability coverage. Accusations are explicit absent/inconsistent predicates, including those in partial findings. Unknown unresolved predicates are not accusations. Zero denominators are `null` (undefined/inconclusive), never perfect. The proposed dev target flag requires useful sample denominators; it grants no product or comparative acceptance. Empty, all-unknown and all-missing systems cannot satisfy the complete target set.

All-atom `extraction_precision`/`extraction_recall`, omission/failure counts and addressable share remain unchanged. `supported_extraction_precision`/`supported_extraction_recall` additionally use the same one-to-one valid matcher over the static kinds in `audit.policy.SUPPORTED_KINDS`. Counts expose the supported match/prediction totals and each metric's explicit numerator/denominator. Duplicated/extra/invalid supported predictions remain in its precision denominator; omitted supported gold stays in recall. Zero supported denominators remain null. Only the extraction thresholds in `dev_targets_met` use these supported metrics, at the existing .95/.90 limits; all other thresholds and sample floors are unchanged. This pre-lock policy alignment is source only, with no measured/tuned score.

| Extraction metric | Numerator | Denominator | Dev extraction threshold |
|---|---|---|---|
| `extraction_precision` | `valid_matched_atoms` | `atom_predictions` | Descriptive all-atom output |
| `extraction_recall` | `valid_matched_atoms` | `gold_atoms` | Descriptive all-atom output |
| `supported_extraction_precision` | `supported_extraction_precision_numerator` | `supported_extraction_precision_denominator` | Existing .95 limit |
| `supported_extraction_recall` | `supported_extraction_recall_numerator` | `supported_extraction_recall_denominator` | Existing .90 limit |

The scorer produces matched four-way confusion and all-atom item rows, plus extraction precision/recall by kind. It constructs deterministic 95% percentile intervals by resampling whole families, including every member's omissions, failures and extra predictions. Repeated family draws receive separate sample identities. Intervals are exploratory; fewer than six families or an undefined denominator in any replicate produces an inconclusive interval. This source has not generated any measured interval.

`evaluation.run_packet_grep(packet, snapshot, root)` is an independent literal/path comparator. It does not call candidate inventory or inspect hidden source for inference. Positive exact path presence is decided only when that path has an admitted excerpt whose pointer reopens through the sole citation service. Symbols, negated paths and absent grep tokens remain unknown without a complete supported proof. The helper returns explicit prediction rows and positive excerpt evidence in `PacketBaseline`; its versioned literal-path method is distinct from full-index query replay. Fenced examples are excluded as nonassertions. It does not run automatically during preparation, and no comparison has been measured.

`evaluation.summarize_attempts(attempts)` totals actual `Attempt` observations. Unknown tokens/cost remain `null`; reported known cost is separately labeled. Unknown latency leaves overall median/p95 undefined rather than treating missing observations as zero. The closed-run score path uses this summary from reopened `RUN.json`; raw prediction-row input has no attached run measurements. Rules, replay and live mode counts remain distinct. No provider call is made by the helper.

`evaluation.run_paired` provides source-only fake/replay assembly of candidate, independent grep/path, extraction-proposal and single-pass roles over identical canonical packet bytes. It records complete response/attempt/failure rows, uses the existing sole citation resolver and deterministic finding policy, preserves fresh output directories and atomically publishes close metadata. Optional full-index utility is a separate treatment. The provider request carries no rule-extracted candidates or labels. See [paired arm API and limitations](paired-arms.md). One shared packet-fact authority uses complete admitted Python/TOML files for scoped declarations, literals, dependency/metadata/CLI declarations, literal environment references and named bounded-code patterns. Partial files, dynamic scopes and ambiguous namespaces abstain; absent packet text never proves absence. New exact-span model atoms use raw assertion-context validation rather than a rules-inventory admission list.

`evaluation.verify_paired(run_root, snapshot_root, *, manifest_digest=None)` reopens closed paired artifact/packet/row identities and admitted citations, checks physical attempts against durable events/budgets, and replays the packet fact/policy proof. It returns verified source metadata plus per-arm prediction/attempt objects; it is distinct from `citations.verify_report` for a full-index `AuditReport`. Optional external manifest pinning detects a changed manifest; an unpinned self-consistent bundle does not prove origin. `evaluation.score_paired(run_root, snapshot_root, gold_path, out, *, manifest_digest=None)` verifies first, then reads one snapshot's curator family and scores all arm rows including pipeline failures. It writes descriptive per-arm outputs in a fresh directory outside the inference run/source. No score, provider inference, verifier execution or runtime integration has been performed during source construction.

No `LOCK.json`, human gold or measured results are checked into this source construction. An unadmitted template is not an admitted experiment. Actual human/live comparisons, locked-corpus paired inference/scoring integration, provider accounting correctness and normal runtime validation remain outstanding.

`run_live_experiment(protocol_path, snapshot_root, out, *, protocol_digest, caps, system_prompts, models, authorizations=None, transports=None, adapters=None, ledger_root=None, budget_admission=None, settlement_observers=None)` now supplies dormant source for required extraction/single-pass experiment orchestration. It rejects missing/disabled grants before control/source access, checks the externally pinned admitted LOCK and original rights/custody/code/version guards, and admits all source/scope paths before source-file reads. It builds one shared raw packet and calls only D4's `produce_live` with owner-injected adapters/transports and the existing ledger; it constructs no clients or credentials. Both treatments use equal locked total caps, with one logical single-pass request plus at most one retry. Unknown usage/cost settlement halts subsequent experiment dispatch and retains incomplete/unattempted arm rows. Real provenance requires the published HTTP transport and an explicit durable `ledger_root` outside source/output, scoped by protocol/packet/role; a fresh output cannot implicitly reset the same pending liability. Producer ledger views are copied into the same closed artifacts for verification. Fixture injection remains explicitly fake and may use output-local ledgers; full-index utility stays separate.

The returned dict contains `preparation_root`, `paired_root`, and `manifest`. The existing verifier/scorer consumes `paired_root` for either fixture-port or live-protocol provenance. Protocol/prompt/grant/request/payload identities, usage origin, attempt counts and all failure rows remain explicit; neither source nor a real future response can automatically mark human or comparative acceptance complete. After closure, single-packet locked scoring additionally requires the actual raw gold JSONL digest to match the admitted `gold_seal_digest`. Corpus-wide aggregate seal/interval/admission work remains outside this single-packet adapter. See [dormant experiment templates](../config/experiments/README.md); no operation in these APIs or templates has been performed now.

Real HTTP additionally requires the same content-bound `LiveBudgetAdmission` on the owner transport and durable root, binding protocol, packet, caps and both role authorization IDs. Optional settlement observers must be the already injected owner observers. Pricing, worst-case liability and receipt-backed reconciliation use the single accounting/provider authority; missing observer or admitted dated rates remains unknown and stops later dispatch. Derived settlement cost is distinct from an owner-supplied provider bill. No rates, receipts, ledger or provider operations were executed during construction.
